---
layout: "layouts/marketing.hbs"
permalink: "/"
tags:
  - "publicPage"
title: "Home"
style: "home"
imageLightbox: true
description: "Send email from Java with a small fluent API and built-in support for security, shared configuration, diagnostics, conversion, batching, clusters, and proxies."
---
    <section class="hero home-hero">
      <div class="shell hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <h1>Simple to use.<br><em>Built for the real world.</em></h1>
          <p class="hero-lede">Send a well-formed email with a small fluent API. Add security, pooled delivery, recovery and observability as your application needs them, and inspect supplied messages through the same library.</p>
          <div class="button-row">
            <a class="button button-primary" href="/download.html">Send your first email <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
            <a class="text-link" href="#start-small">See what it handles <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="code-window hero-code" aria-label="Minimal Simple Java Mail example">
          <div class="code-window-bar">
            <span>FirstSend.java</span>
            <span class="hero-version">v{{site.version}}</span>
          </div>
          <pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("Sender", "sender@example.org")
    .to("Recipient", "recipient@example.net")
    .withSubject("It works")
    .withPlainText("Your first message.")
    .buildEmail();

try (Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer(
        System.getenv("SMTP_HOST"), 587,
        System.getenv("SMTP_USER"),
        System.getenv("SMTP_PASSWORD"))
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
    .buildMailer()) {
    mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
          <div class="smtp-receipt">
            <span class="receipt-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span>SMTP submission</span>
            <code>250 2.0.0 queued</code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="proof-strip" aria-label="Project facts">
      <div class="shell proof-grid">
        <div><span>Current release</span><strong>v{{site.version}}</strong><small>{{site.releaseDate}}</small></div>
        <div><span>Runtime</span><strong>{{site.javaBaseline}}</strong><small>Source and target</small></div>
        <div><span>License</span><strong>{{site.license}}</strong><small>Open source</small></div>
        <div><span>Mail foundation</span><strong>Jakarta Mail API</strong><small>Angus included by default</small></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="progression ruled-section" aria-labelledby="start-small">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">The same API as needs grow</p>
        <h2 id="start-small">Start small without starting over.</h2>
        <p class="progression-intro">
          <strong>Simple Java Mail grows with you from that first announcement to a shared mail service that feeds back into your business workflows.</strong>
        </p>
        <ol class="progression-steps">
          <li>
            <div class="progression-row">
              <p>You start with a simple marketing email announcing a new product.</p>
              <div class="progression-api">
                {{> components/captain-journey-panel panel="first-send" label="First send" alt="Captain winks beside a single floating envelope."}}
                <div class="progression-api-copy">
                  <span>Send the announcement</span>
                  <code>mailer.sync().sendMail(email)</code>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="progression-row">
              <p>
                Soon you’re sending regular campaigns. You set up
                {{#with (capability "dkim" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">DKIM</a>{{/with}}
                once on the mailer so each message goes out signed for your domain.
              </p>
              <div class="progression-api">
                {{> components/captain-journey-panel panel="dkim" label="DKIM" alt="Captain’s emblem glows as a blue postmark appears on the envelope."}}
                <div class="progression-api-copy">
                  <span>Sign each campaign</span>
                  <code>.signWithDomainKey(dkimConfig)</code>
                  <code>.withEmailDefaults(dkimDefaults)</code>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="progression-row">
              <p>
                As the list grows into the thousands, you
                {{#with (capability "pools" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">reuse SMTP connections</a>{{/with}}
                and {{#with (capability "sending-limits" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">pace sends</a>{{/with}}
                to stay within your provider’s limits.
              </p>
              <div class="progression-api">
                {{> components/captain-journey-panel panel="campaign-volume" label="Campaign volume" alt="A teal ring of evenly spaced envelopes orbits Captain."}}
                <div class="progression-api-copy">
                  <span>Send to a bigger list</span>
                  <code>.withConnectionPoolConfig(pool)</code>
                  <code>.withSendingLimitsConfig(limits)</code>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="progression-row">
              <p>
                Some messages bounce, so you set a
                {{#with (capability "bounce" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">bounce address</a>{{/with}}
                to route delivery reports to a dedicated mailbox. When your SMTP server clearly refuses a whole send for a temporary reason,
                you {{#with (capability "retries" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">let the mailer retry</a>{{/with}}
                within the limits you set.
              </p>
              <div class="progression-api">
                {{> components/captain-journey-panel panel="bounces" label="Bounces" alt="Captain catches a returning envelope as a red aura flares around him."}}
                <div class="progression-api-copy">
                  <span>Handle bounces and temporary refusals</span>
                  <code>.withBounceTo(bounceAddress)</code>
                  <code>.withMailSendRetryPolicy(retry)</code>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="progression-row">
              <p>
                Then billing and account security start using the same service. You give invoices and password resets
                <a href="/email-workload-field-guide.html#login-codes-behind-a-newsletter">their own pool</a>
                so they have room to send during a big campaign. You add a
                {{#with (capability "failover" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">backup relay</a>{{/with}}
                in case the primary is unreachable.
              </p>
              <div class="progression-api">
                {{> components/captain-journey-panel panel="urgent-mail" label="Urgent mail" alt="Captain’s gold aura parts the campaign mail for a glowing urgent envelope."}}
                <div class="progression-api-copy">
                  <span>Give urgent mail its own route</span>
                  <code>.withClusterKey(urgentCluster)</code>
                  <code>.withFailoverMailer(backup, policy)</code>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="progression-row">
              <p>
                With several jobs running at once, you use each send’s
                {{#with (capability "progress" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">observation ID</a>{{/with}}
                to match its live progress to its final result. If an invoice
                <a href="/email-workload-field-guide.html#the-invoice-was-accepted-then-bounced">bounces later</a>
                and the report includes the <a href="/delivery-reports.html#correlate-and-decide">SMTP envelope ID</a>,
                your app can match it to the waiting billing job and flag the right invoice for follow-up.
              </p>
              <div class="progression-api">
                {{> components/captain-journey-panel panel="job-matching" label="Job matching" alt="A glowing line connects a bounce report to its job across Captain’s galaxy of envelopes."}}
                <div class="progression-api-copy">
                  <span>Match results to send jobs</span>
                  <code>progress.getObservationId()</code>
                  <code>receipt.getEnvelopeId()</code>
                  <code>report.getEnvelopeId()</code>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <a class="text-link progression-pattern-link" href="/patterns/mail-workflow.html">
          Run the mail feedback example <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="delivery-route" class="home-capabilities ruled-section" data-pagefind-ignore>
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">What it handles</p><h2>From the first email to the difficult requirements.</h2></header>
        <div class="capability-summary">
          {{#each (capabilityGroups @root.capabilities)}}
            <article>
              <h3><a href="{{guide}}">{{label}} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></h3>
              <p>{{#each highlights}}<a href="{{href}}">{{label}}</a>{{#unless @last}} · {{/unless}}{{/each}}</p>
            </article>
          {{/each}}
        </div>
        <a class="text-link" href="/features.html">All capabilities <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rare-requirements">
      <div class="shell rare-grid">
        <header class="rare-heading">
          <p class="eyebrow">Less common, still supported</p>
          <h2>The edge cases are part of the design.</h2>
          <p>Most applications never need these. The ones that do should not have to replace their mail library or invent infrastructure around it.</p>
        </header>

        <article class="rare-item">
          <div class="network-glyph cluster-glyph" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Many servers, one mailer</p>
          {{> components/module-badges batch=true}}
          {{#with (capability "clusters" @root.capabilities)}}<h3>{{label}}</h3>{{/with}}
          <p>Give each workload its own connection pool and set of SMTP servers. That can isolate tenants, use more than one provider, or keep transactional mail separate from batch work.</p>
          {{#with (capability "clusters" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">Configure clusters <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>{{/with}}
        </article>

        <article class="rare-item">
          <div class="network-glyph proxy-glyph" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><b></b></div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Network constraints</p>
          {{> components/module-badges socks=true}}
          {{#with (capability "proxy" @root.capabilities)}}<h3>{{label}}</h3>{{/with}}
          <p>Send through a SOCKS proxy that requires a username and password without changing the mailer configuration model.</p>
          {{#with (capability "proxy" @root.capabilities)}}<a href="{{href}}">Configure a proxy <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>{{/with}}
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    {{#each collections.caseStudies}}
      {{#if data.caseStudy.spotlight}}
        <section class="home-case-study ruled-section" aria-label="Featured case study">
          <div class="shell">
            {{> components/case-study-spotlight this id="home-relaydesk"}}
          </div>
        </section>
      {{/if}}
    {{/each}}

    <section class="comparison-teaser ruled-section">
      <div class="shell comparison-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Compare your options</p>
          <h2>The difference is how much code you still have to write.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>Jakarta Mail gives you the protocol and message foundation. Spring Mail adds Spring's standard sender contract. Commons Email shortens some basic message code. Simple Java Mail builds well-formed MIME and adds reusable message rules, DKIM, S/MIME, OpenPGP/MIME, diagnostics, conversion, authenticated SOCKS, and batched, pooled, or clustered delivery.</p>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/feature-matrix.html">Compare the approaches <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="install-band" id="install">
      <div class="shell install-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Ready when you are</p>
          <h2>Add one dependency.</h2>
          <p>Start with <code>simple-java-mail</code>. Add another module only when you need features such as DKIM, S/MIME, OpenPGP/MIME, pooling, or Spring integration.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="install-dependency">
          {{~> components/dependency-picker}}
          <a class="button button-light" href="/download.html#first-email">Send your first email <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
